q: calcvar.c -- variables
spec: Grow `calcexp.c` so the calculator remembers values. Everything from
spec: `calcexp` still holds, with the program name `calcvar` in the
spec: messages. A line is now either an expression, as before, or an
spec: assignment, `NAME = EXPRESSION`: the first token is a name and the
spec: second is `=`. Anything else is an expression.
spec:
spec: A name is a letter or underscore followed by letters, digits and
spec: underscores, at most 31 characters, case sensitive. Longer is
spec: `error: col C: name too long` at its start, found with the other
spec: lexical errors. `=` becomes a token of its own.
spec:
spec: An expression may use names. A line that is just an expression
spec: prints its value as before. An assignment evaluates the right side
spec: first, then stores the value under the name (making it if new), and
spec: prints `NAME = VALUE`, for example `x = 12`. A failed line changes
spec: nothing. The reference keeps room for 8 variables.
spec:
spec: New errors, in the same `error: col C: MESSAGE` form:
spec: - `undefined name 'NAME'`, at the start of a name that has no value
spec:   when it is used (in `y = y + 1` the right-hand `y` fails).
spec: - `too many variables`, at the name on the left, when a ninth new
spec:   name would be stored. It comes after the right side was
spec:   evaluated, so a mistake on the right is reported first.
spec: Leftover tokens still give `unexpected input`, so `x = y = 3` fails
spec: at the second `=`.
