q: calcrun.c -- run a program
spec: The last step: the calculator becomes a language you run. Read the
spec: whole of standard input as a program, one statement per line, and
spec: run it. Everything from `calcvar` still holds (names, arithmetic,
spec: the same messages), with the program name `calcrun` in the messages,
spec: except where this says otherwise.
spec:
spec: A hash starts a comment: the line ends there (so a hash never
spec: counts as an unexpected character). A line with nothing left on it
spec: is skipped, but still counts in line numbers, which start at 1.
spec:
spec: A statement is one of:
spec: - `NAME = EXPRESSION`: store the value, print nothing.
spec: - `print EXPRESSION`: print the value on its own line.
spec: A bare expression is no longer a statement. `print` is a keyword
spec: and cannot be a variable name.
spec:
spec: The first error ends the run. It prints one line,
spec: `error: line L, col C: MESSAGE`, and nothing after it runs. The
spec: exit status is 1 then, otherwise 0. The messages are those of
spec: `calcvar`, plus two new ones at column 1 of the statement:
spec: - `not a statement`: the line starts with neither `print` nor a
spec:   name followed by `=`.
spec: - `print is reserved`: an attempt to assign to `print`.
spec: A missing value at the end of a line, such as a lone `print`, is
spec: reported at the column of the statement's first token.
